WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.880
The first film showed the kidney taking back almost all filtered water.

2
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.660
The last litres are the hardest.

3
00:00:11.833 --> 00:00:17.593
A water-deprived adult can make urine about four times as concentrated as plasma.

4
00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:20.933
Yet no pump in the kidney moves water.

5
00:00:22.193 --> 00:00:24.273
So how does it pull water out of such urine?

6
00:00:25.333 --> 00:00:30.713
Concentration here means osmolality, the number of dissolved particles in each

7
00:00:30.713 --> 00:00:31.753
kilogram of water.

8
00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:36.733
Water moves by osmosis, toward the more concentrated side.

9
00:00:37.493 --> 00:00:41.913
So the tissue outside must be a little more concentrated than the urine.

10
00:00:43.213 --> 00:00:46.053
Salt supplies most, and deep down, urea helps.

11
00:00:47.117 --> 00:00:48.737
Picture a slice of kidney.

12
00:00:50.017 --> 00:00:51.517
The outer layer is the cortex.

13
00:00:52.977 --> 00:00:56.417
Beneath lies the medulla, an outer zone above an inner zone.

14
00:00:57.617 --> 00:00:59.837
It ends at the papilla, where urine drains.

15
00:01:01.217 --> 00:01:03.977
A hairpin tube, the loop of Henle, dips into it.

16
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:06.640
Start with the loop's upward arm.

17
00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.060
In the outer medulla it is thick, the thick ascending limb.

18
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.800
It moves salt out, but its wall lets almost no water through.

19
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.300
Salt leaves, water stays, and the tissue gets saltier.

20
00:01:21.333 --> 00:01:24.293
Now the thin descending limb, the downward arm.

21
00:01:25.553 --> 00:01:28.973
In rabbit tubules, it lets water through but holds its salt.

22
00:01:30.633 --> 00:01:35.953
Passing salty tissue, it loses water by osmosis, so its fluid concentrates on the

23
00:01:35.953 --> 00:01:36.953
way down.

24
00:01:37.517 --> 00:01:40.517
The two arms carry fluid in opposite directions.

25
00:01:41.937 --> 00:01:46.337
Concentrated fluid rounds the bend, so the ascending limb works from a saltier

26
00:01:46.337 --> 00:01:50.677
start. Level by level, a modest difference adds up.

27
00:01:51.837 --> 00:01:53.877
This is the countercurrent multiplier.

28
00:01:55.317 --> 00:01:58.277
Deeper down, how the rise continues is still debated.

29
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.620
Suppose the thick ascending limb let water through as easily as the descending limb.

30
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:07.560
Would the multiplier still work well?

31
00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.020
Pause and decide.

32
00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:22.367
Badly weakened. Water would follow the salt out, and most of the difference across

33
00:02:22.367 --> 00:02:23.467
the wall would collapse.

34
00:02:24.847 --> 00:02:26.787
Salt moving without water is the trick.

35
00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.030
Is real tissue graded like this?

36
00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:36.730
Wirz, Hargitay and Kuhn measured the freezing point of thin rat kidney slices from

37
00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:41.150
different depths. More particles mean a lower freezing point.

38
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:45.070
Osmolality rose from cortex toward papilla.

39
00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:51.250
The collecting duct runs from cortex to papilla through ever more concentrated

40
00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:56.290
tissue. Antidiuretic hormone, ADH, acts on its cells.

41
00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:01.690
They insert stored water channels, aquaporin-2, facing the urine.

42
00:03:03.133 --> 00:03:07.593
Water leaves the urine by osmosis into the concentrated medulla.

43
00:03:08.893 --> 00:03:10.513
The urine comes out concentrated.

44
00:03:11.813 --> 00:03:16.013
Take ADH away, the channels withdraw, and dilute urine flows out.

45
00:03:17.017 --> 00:03:19.057
So the kidney never pumps water.

46
00:03:20.297 --> 00:03:24.397
It moves solute first, building a medulla more concentrated than blood.

47
00:03:25.817 --> 00:03:27.977
Water follows where ADH opens the way.
